.TH NOTIFY 2
.SH NAME
notify, noted, note, notepg \- handle and post notes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "notify :: proc(handler: uintptr) -> i64"
.B "noted :: proc(how: u64) -> !"
.B "note :: proc(pid: u64, text: string) -> i64"
.B "notepg :: proc(pid: u64, text: string) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
A
.I note
is a string posted to a process from outside it.
With no handler registered, a process that receives a note ends,
.I wait
answering
.B EINTR
for it.
.PP
.I Notify
registers
.I handler
as the address a note is delivered to, replacing any previous handler.
An address of zero clears the handler, restoring the default action.
.I Notify
is refused with
.B EBUSY
while a delivery is already in progress.
.PP
On delivery, the note's text and a copy of the interrupted machine state
are pushed onto the program's own stack, and control transfers to
.I handler
with the address of the saved state as its first argument and the
address of the NUL-terminated text as its second.
The handler runs on the same stack the note interrupted; a stack with no
room for the delivery ends the process, as if no handler had been
registered.
No floating-point state crosses a delivery.
.PP
A handler must end by calling
.IR noted .
.B abi.NCONT
restores the saved state and resumes the interrupted program from it,
after which an interrupted system call answers
.BR EINTR .
.B abi.NDFLT
takes the default action, the ending a note always is.
Neither value returns to the handler.
A note posted while a handler is running is held and delivered at the
first opportunity after
.I noted
finishes.
.PP
.I Note
posts
.I text
to one of the caller's own children, named by
.IR pid .
.I Notepg
posts
.I text
to every process in a note group but the caller: a
.I pid
of zero names the caller's own group, and any other
.I pid
must be one of the caller's children, whose group is then the target.
A child forked without
.B RFNOTEG
(see
.IR fork (2))
is in its parent's group; one forked with the flag is in a group of one.
.I Notepg
answers how many processes were posted to, which is zero for a group
with nobody else in it.
.PP
Unlike Plan 9, a program that faults does not receive a note: it ends
directly, and its parent's
.I wait
answers
.BR EIO .
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/notify.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR wait (2),
.IR proc (3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Answers zero, or the count posted, or a negative
.B \-errno ;
see
.IR errstr (2).
.B ECHILD
is a
.I pid
that is not the caller's own child.
